#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# knapsack_solve

#% text
# `knapsack_solve(items, cap)` gives the number of copies of each item that maximize the total payoff within the total cost `cap`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# An item is a cost (payoff 1), `[payoff, cost]` or `[payoff, cost, max_count]`, or a list of rules with the keys "Payoff", "Cost" and "MaxCount".

#% item
# Items given as an association or as a list of `name -> item` rules give the counts in the same form.

#% item
# The problem is solved exactly by dynamic programming over the costs (decimal costs are scaled to integers); a capacity above 200000 scaled units leaves the call unevaluated.

#% item
# Several optimal solutions exist often; one of them is returned.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
knapsack_solve([4, 2, 3], 8)
#% output
#= [0, 4, 0]

#% cell
knapsack_solve([[3, 4], [2, 5]], 10)
#% output
#= [2, 0]

#% cell
knapsack_solve([[3, 2, 7], [2, 1, 5]], 10)
#% output
#= [3, 4]

#% cell
knapsack_solve(assoc("apples" -> [8, 3, 3], "oranges" -> [9, 7, 5]), 25)
#% output
#= assoc("apples" -> 3, "oranges" -> 2)

#% cell
knapsack_solve([["Payoff" -> 4, "Cost" -> 12], ["Payoff" -> 5, "Cost" -> 13, "MaxCount" -> 2]], 45)
#% output
#= [1, 2]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `linear_optimization`, `frobenius_solve`
